rity  forces are still not able to defend  their own territory you've had  consecutive administrations you just  played all those sound bites two  american presidents wanted out two  american administrations decided that  having american troops in afghanistan  was not in the national security  interest of the united states and the  american people are first of all tired  of it they don't want american troops  there they also don't understand what  we're doing there yeah just in the last  few minutes we got word that secretary  blinken and secretary austin state and  defense secretaries had a call with the  afghan president ashraf ghani they are  saying that they are committed to the  state stability and security of  afghanistan ben but they're also  privately shooting down  stories or rumors that they asked ghani  to resign  thoughts about this as it's evolving  just over the past couple days  you know i'm a child of the 80's brett  and uh and i'm my siblings uh and my  brothers-in-law have spent far more time  in afghanistan than anyone on this panel  and i was talking to my brother right  before i came on and i said what what  would you like me to say and he said  what do you expect what do you expect  after all of this okay a policy that was  misbegotten in terms of its anticipation  of trying to achieve things in  afghanistan that may be unachievable  blood that cries out from the ground  there i think in terms of wasted  resources and lives when it came to  american policy and i think a failed  definition of victory  in so many different respects and across  multiple and bipartisan administrations  we've had three presidents in a row  elected based on arguing against the  wars in the middle east and in  afghanistan it seems to me that the  neoconservative agenda that cares so  much about democracy overseas is not  very interested in listening to it here  at home and i think instead what we  ought to be asking is if this was  something that we wanted to achieve 